# Issue #962, execution twin of `heritage/check/heritage-value-of-key-reader-across-a-newline`.
#
# The check twin proves the newline spellings are not DIAGNOSED. That is the weaker half. The dict
# rows were diagnosed AND misread: the broken reading produced a dict holding a single entry keyed
# `key` whose value was a call to a procedure `k`, with the entry `a` gone — and the `ol-bad-token`
# it raised described an incomplete reader, never the wrong tree. A fix could therefore have removed
# the diagnostics and kept the misreading, and the check twin would still have passed.
#
# So every pair below prints the newline spelling and then its one-line twin, and the two `print`
# events of a pair must carry identical payloads. That identity is the runtime witness of "the
# newline spelling means what its one-line spelling means"; the exact tree is asserted next door, in
# `packages/parser/src/value-of-key-newline.test.mjs`.
#
# `execute()` runs `parse()` and never `check()`, so the runtime holds its own copy of these rules.
# The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs Data (`spec/conformance.md:305`).
:d = { a: 7 b: "a" }
:k = "a"

# Bare.
print value of :d
for key :k
print value of :d for key :k

# Parenthesized — the spelling whose parentheses were reported unmatched.
print (value of :d
for key :k)
print (value of :d for key :k)

# In a list literal.
print [value of :d
for key :k]
print [value of :d for key :k]

# In a dict literal. Both members must print a dict keyed `a` holding 7 — under the defect the
# first printed a dict keyed `key` instead, with no `a` in it at all.
print { a: value of :d
for key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# Newline after `of`, at the top level and then inside a dict entry value.
print value of
:d for key :k
print value of :d for key :k
print { a: value of
:d for key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# Newline between `for` and `key`, at the top level and then inside a dict entry value.
print value of :d for
key :k
print value of :d for key :k
print { a: value of :d for
key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# Newline after `key`, at the top level and then inside a dict entry value.
print value of :d for key
:k
print value of :d for key :k
print { a: value of :d for key
:k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# A newline on BOTH sides of `for`, at the top level and then inside a dict entry value. The tail's
# two words are looked up past newlines of their own, so this is still one expression.
print value of :d
for
key :k
print value of :d for key :k
print { a: value of :d
for
key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# Case is the third axis of the cross-product: keywords are case-insensitive
# (`spec/grammar.md:13`), so an UPPERCASE tail must continue across a newline and mean exactly what
# the lowercase one means. `style` is a check-only flag that `execute()` never reads, so it has no
# bearing here either way; the tail words `of`/`for`/`key` are not linted at all in any case.
print value of :d
FOR KEY :k
print value of :d for key :k
print { a: value of :d
FOR key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# Blank lines and a comment line are whitespace inside the expression like any other — pinned here
# in the execution direction, and inside a dict entry value, where the check twin covers only the
# top level.
print { a: value of :d

# a comment between the operand and the tail

for key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# A nested reader whose inner reader is itself split across a newline.
print value of (value of { inner: :d }
for key "inner")
for key "a"
print value of (value of { inner: :d } for key "inner") for key "a"
# Control: a newline still terminates a statement, so these stay TWO instructions and two prints.
print 1
print 2
